- op: discuss  # 4cc9
  at: '2026-09-17T04:36:06Z'  # 4cc9
  by: agent  # 4cc9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4cc9
  clock: 3  # 4cc9
  nonce: 4cc9  # 4cc9
  message: "Done in PR #1018 (dev fb3a0fb93d). prerequisite_absent has a producer, and the reason to build it was not the empty producer -- it was that the axis was asserting something FALSE in its place.\n\nACCEPTANCE TEST: PASSED, but NOT as this row specifies it, and the difference decides the verdict. Run literally, the test in this row and in ADR-0056 comes back EMPTY -- and this row says an empty result flips the verdict to DEPRECATE. It would have deprecated a value that is demonstrably firing. Two faults in the test, both checkable:\n\n  (1) ~/repos/aardvark-dns does not exist; the repo is in ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/ (the two-roots hazard). Worse, it is RUST-ONLY -- 16 .rs files, ZERO .js -- so it is not the 'JS+Rust repo' the test calls it. tauri-ipc-linker and wasm-bindgen-linker are activation-gated OFF there and produce no AnalysisRun at all, so the second assertion cannot be satisfied by construction.\n\n  (2) Removing tree-sitter-language-pack does not remove rust. I did exactly that and  RAN. The mainstream grammars come from DEDICATED wheels (rust.py: grammar_module = 'tree_sitter_rust'); the language pack is the long tail's fallback. The catalog says otherwise for 25 of 26 grammar-gated literals -- Pass.requires is wrong, nothing but humans reads it, and it is what aimed the test at the wrong package. Filed as WI-pakof.\n\nRE-AIMED AND DECISIVE. Uninstall tree-sitter-rust; survey a genuine JS+Rust repo (component-model-demo, and robyn as a second). Both reproduce:\n  limits.skipped_passes: {'pass': 'rust', 'reason': 'rust tree-sitter grammar not available'} -- a TOOLCHAIN reason, with 2 and 27 .rs files present.\n  tauri-ipc-linker and rust-trait-dispatch-linker: silent, and claiming no_candidate_files -- declared to mean 'nothing to find, and no ordering or declaration mechanism would change it'. Installing the grammar changes it. THE AXIS WAS PUBLISHING A CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER.\n\nSo the verdict is KEEP AND BUILD, not DEPRECATE, and the evidence is stronger than this row anticipated: the value's absence is not a gap, it is a falsehood.\n\nTHE RULE AS SHIPPED matches this row exactly (every literal of some conjunct absent AND at least one skipped with a code other than no_candidate_files), with two things this row did not specify:\n\n  (a) UNREPORTED counts as a toolchain reason. A producer that did not classify itself has not said the repo lacked the files; waving it through as State A would read 'cannot determine' as 'not applicable'.\n\n  (b) IT ONLY FILLS A FIELD THE BODY LEFT EMPTY, and the dry run is why. Four silent runs matched the blocked conjunct; TWO of them carry a BODY claim. pyffi-linker says no_candidate_construct truthfully -- it scanned the Python side and there are genuinely no FFI call sites, so the missing Rust grammar is irrelevant to ITS silence. Overruling it would reverse PR #1008's guard and replace a true claim with a plausible one. prerequisite_absent therefore UNDER-REPORTS by design, and it can only see ANALYZER prerequisites since linkers are deliberately not enumerated in skipped_passes.\n\nMEASURED before/after on the same repository: tauri-ipc-linker and rust-trait-dispatch-linker move no_candidate_files -> prerequisite_absent; pyffi-linker and wasm-bindgen-linker keep their body claims. Survey stderr now reads 'prerequisite_absent=2'."  # 4cc9
